            "title": "From identity to permissions",
            "body": "Knowing *who* you are, the next question is *what you may do*. AWS permissions come from **IAM policies** attached to users, groups and roles. If your principal can read IAM (`iam:ListAttachedUserPolicies`, `iam:GetPolicyVersion`, `iam:ListGroupsForUser`), you can simply *read* your own permissions. But often you cannot read IAM, so you must **infer** permissions by trying actions and watching for `AccessDenied` versus success. Worked example: `aws iam list-attached-user-policies --user-name deploy-bot` may return `AdministratorAccess` (jackpot) or `AccessDenied` (now you must brute-force). Predict: if you cannot read your own IAM policy, how do you learn your permissions? ||By probing actions and observing which succeed versus return AccessDenied, i.e. permission brute-forcing.||",
