            "title": "Why a graph beats a list",
            "body": "A list of permissions tells you facts; a **graph** tells you *paths*. The core insight of modern cloud (and Active Directory) attacking is that access is a **network of relationships**, and attackers think in edges, not entries. Analogy: a map of individual buildings is far less useful than one showing the roads between them, because you want the *route*. Model the cloud as **nodes** (principals like users and roles, and resources like buckets, secrets and functions) joined by **edges** (permissions like *can-assume*, *can-read-secret*, *can-pass-role*). Once it is a graph, 'how do I become admin?' becomes a **shortest-path** problem a computer can solve.",
