            "title": "Why permissions are the whole game",
            "body": "In a data center you break in by stealing a password or exploiting a server. In AWS there is no 'server to own' first — every action is gated by **IAM** (Identity and Access Management), which answers one question for every API call: *is this principal allowed to do this action on this resource?* **Privilege escalation** is turning a weak set of permissions into a stronger set, legitimately, using the permissions you already hold. Think of a mailroom clerk who is allowed to *print new ID badges*: they can simply print themselves a CEO badge. The permission to make badges was the whole vulnerability.",
