            "title": "The core idea: borrowing power through a service",
            "body": "Services need roles to do their jobs — a Lambda function uses a role to read S3, an EC2 instance uses one to call other APIs. **`iam:PassRole`** is the permission to *give a role to a service* when you create that resource. If you can pass a high-privilege role to a service you control, the service runs YOUR instructions with THAT role's permissions. Analogy: you cannot enter the vault, but you are allowed to assign which armored-truck the bank uses. So you assign the truck that already has vault keys, then tell it where to drive. The power was never yours — you borrowed the service's hands.",
            "check": {
              "question": "What does PassRole-based escalation fundamentally exploit?",
              "options": [
                "A bug in the AWS STS service",
                "A compute service executing your code under a role you were allowed to pass to it",
                "A weak IAM user password"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "You never hold the role's keys directly — the service runs as the role on your behalf."
            }
