            "title": "The confused-deputy problem",
            "body": "A **confused deputy** is a trusted middle-man tricked into using its authority for an attacker. Classic AWS case: a SaaS vendor's account assumes a role in *your* account to do its job. If the vendor serves many customers and you only check that 'the vendor's account may assume', then attacker-customer-B can ask the vendor to act in YOUR account — the vendor is the confused deputy wielding your trust. Analogy: a courier company is allowed into your building; a stranger tells the courier 'grab the package from apartment 4B', and the courier, trusted, does it. The deputy is not malicious — it is confused about on whose behalf it acts.",
