            "title": "OIDC / web-identity trust misconfig",
            "body": "Modern roles are often assumed by **OIDC** identity providers instead of AWS principals — e.g. **GitHub Actions** assumes an AWS role via `token.actions.githubusercontent.com` with no stored secrets. Safety depends entirely on the `Condition` matching the token's **`sub`** (subject) claim, like `repo:my-org/my-repo:ref:refs/heads/main`. A dangerous misconfig uses a wildcard such as `repo:my-org/*` or only checks `aud` — now *any* repo in the org (or in some broken setups, any GitHub repo) can assume your role and reach your account. Analogy: the bouncer checks 'works at our company' but not *which* employee — any insider fits. Worked finding: a trust policy with `\"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub\": \"repo:acme/*\"` lets a fork or unrelated acme repo assume the role.",
