            "title": "ACLs: the legacy layer",
            "body": "**ACLs (Access Control Lists)** are S3's original permission system from 2006. They attach a short list of **grantees** to a bucket or an individual object. Two grantees are dangerous: **AllUsers** means literally anyone on the internet with no login at all, and **AuthenticatedUsers** means *any logged-in AWS account on Earth* — a notorious misunderstanding, because people assume it means only their own organisation. Worked example: granting `READ` to `AuthenticatedUsers` feels safe but the check only asks 'do you hold any AWS account?'. Predict before revealing — who can read an object granted to `AuthenticatedUsers`? ||Any person with any AWS account anywhere, millions of people, not only your company.||",
