            "title": "See the reads: CloudTrail data events",
            "body": "To investigate (or prove) exfiltration you need **object-level** visibility, and this is off by default. **CloudTrail data events** log individual `GetObject`/`PutObject`/`DeleteObject` calls, so you can see exactly which objects a credential read and when. The default CloudTrail trail records *management* events (bucket created, policy changed) but **not** object reads — a common blind spot. Analogy: the building's entry log shows who badged into the lobby, but you need the per-room camera to know which filing cabinets they opened. Worked example: after an incident, data events let you list precisely which 12,000 objects the stolen credential copied. Predict: default CloudTrail is on — does it show that an attacker read 12,000 objects? ||No — you must explicitly enable S3 data events first; management-only logging won't show object reads.||",
