            "title": "The disease: long-lived static keys",
            "body": "Almost every breach in Topics 1-2 traces to one thing: a **long-lived static key** (an AWS access key pair starting `AKIA...`) that was created once and then copied into a `.env`, a CI variable, a laptop, an image. Static keys have no expiry, so a copy leaked three years ago still works today, and they carry no context about *where* they're used, so a stolen key works from anywhere on earth. Predict: you rotate your static keys every 90 days — does that solve the leak problem? ||It shrinks the window but doesn't fix it — a key leaked on day 1 is fully valid for up to 90 days, and rotation is easy to forget; the real fix is to not have a storable key at all.|| The modern goal is **zero long-lived keys**: let the workload prove who it is and hand it credentials that expire in minutes.",
