            "title": "Why federation kills the problem at the root",
            "body": "Step back and see *why* this works. A leaked-key attack needs a key that (a) exists somewhere storable and (b) stays valid long enough to use. Federation removes both: the credential is **minted on demand**, **expires in minutes**, and is **bound to context** (this repo, this pod, this VM) by the trust policy. There is no file, env var, or image layer holding a reusable secret, so the entire Topic-2 harvest comes up empty. Predict: an attacker dumps a GitHub Actions runner's memory mid-job and grabs the temporary AWS creds — how bad is it? ||Bad but bounded — those creds expire in roughly an hour and are scoped to that role, so the blast radius and time window are tiny compared with a static key that works forever; federation turns a catastrophe into a contained incident.|| That bounding is the whole point.",
