            "title": "Security Groups: Stateful, Allow-Only",
            "body": "A **security group (SG)** is a firewall attached directly to an instance's network interface. Two defining traits: it is **allow-only** (you list what's permitted; everything else is denied by default) and it is **stateful** (if you allow a request in, the reply is automatically allowed back out — it 'remembers' the connection). Analogy: a bouncer with a guest list and a memory — once he waves you in, he remembers to let you back out without re-checking. Worked example: you add one rule 'allow TCP 443 from 0.0.0.0/0' to a web server. Predict: do you also need a rule to allow the HTTPS response back out? ||No. Security groups are stateful, so the return traffic for an allowed inbound connection is permitted automatically. You never write return rules.||",
