            "title": "The blind spot CloudTrail can't cover",
            "body": "CloudTrail logs **API calls**, but once a program is running on an instance and opens an ordinary network connection, CloudTrail sees nothing. Analogy: CloudTrail is the record of who signed into the building; it cannot tell you what two people whispered once inside. **VPC Flow Logs** record network connections within your VPC — source, destination, ports, bytes, and whether traffic was **accepted or rejected**. Worked example: an attacker with a shell on your instance uploading a database to an external IP produces **no** telltale API call, but it does produce a flow-log entry showing gigabytes going to an unknown address. Predict: will a reverse shell's traffic appear in CloudTrail? ||No — it is raw network traffic; you need VPC Flow Logs or similar to see it.||",
