            "title": "Stage 2 — Discovery: learning what the badge unlocks",
            "body": "A new attacker with a stolen key has no idea what it can do — so they **enumerate**. This is the quiet, patient phase of **trying every door in the building with your one badge and noting which open**. In AWS the opening moves are read-only and noisy-but-normal: `aws sts get-caller-identity` (who am I?), `aws iam get-account-authorization-details` or per-service `list*`/`describe*` calls (what exists and who can touch it?). The goal is to build a mental map: which buckets, which databases, which other roles, and crucially **what this identity is allowed to do**. Worked example: the key belongs to a CI role; `list-attached-role-policies` reveals it has `iam:PassRole` and `lambda:CreateFunction` — a combination the attacker will weaponise two stages from now. ||Discovery is where defenders have their best early signal: a principal that suddenly makes hundreds of describe/list calls across services it never normally touches is a strong anomaly.||",
