            "title": "The defender's north star: shrinking dwell time",
            "body": "Every stage of the chain gives the defender a **chance to detect and a clock that is ticking**. **Dwell time** is how long an attacker operates undetected — from first foothold to eviction. Picture a burglar in your house: whether they steal nothing or everything depends almost entirely on **how long before the alarm sounds**. The defender's whole job across this module is to **push detection earlier in the chain** and **cut dwell time toward zero**, because earlier detection means fewer stages completed and less data lost. Worked example: catching the anomalous AssumeRole at the lateral-movement stage might cost the attacker everything; catching it only at the exfiltration stage means the data is already gone. Every control we study — GuardDuty, CloudTrail baselines, Access Analyzer — exists to move that moment of detection to the left. ||Reducing dwell time is usually more achievable, and more valuable, than trying to achieve perfect prevention at initial access.||",
