            "title": "Resource policies: cross-account access without AssumeRole",
            "body": "Roles are not the only way across a wall. Many services have their **own resource policy** that can directly grant access to a principal in another account — no role assumption needed. An **S3 bucket policy**, a **KMS key policy**, an **SNS/SQS policy**, or an **ECR repository policy** can each say 'account X may read/use me'. Think of it as **a specific door in the safe that was built to open from the neighbour's side**. This matters because such access can be **invisible if you only audit IAM roles** — the grant lives on the *resource*, not on any identity. Worked example: a KMS key policy that allows `kms:Decrypt` to an external account means that account can decrypt anything protected by that key, even data it was never supposed to see. ||A frequent audit blind spot: teams review IAM roles carefully but forget that S3, KMS, SNS, SQS, and ECR resource policies can independently grant cross-account access.||",
