            "title": "The classic pivot: EC2 -> metadata role -> data",
            "body": "The most iconic cloud pivot is from a compromised **EC2 instance** to the **IAM role attached to it**, and from there to the data that role can read. Every instance can query the **Instance Metadata Service (IMDS)** at the link-local address `169.254.169.254` to get its **temporary role credentials** — a convenience so apps don't store keys. But if an attacker runs code on the instance (or triggers **SSRF** so the app fetches metadata for them), they steal those credentials. It is like **a company car whose glovebox holds a key to headquarters** — steal the car, open the glovebox, drive to HQ. Worked example (the heart of the 2019 Capital One breach): SSRF forced the server to read `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/<role>`, leaking the role's keys, which could then list and read S3 buckets. ||IMDSv2 defends this by requiring a PUT-obtained session token first, which breaks most simple SSRF reads that only perform a GET.||",
