            "body": "Three things are easily confused. An **SCP** caps permissions for whole *accounts* in an org (org-level ceiling). A **permission boundary** caps the permissions of a *single IAM principal* (user/role) — useful when you let developers create roles but want to bound what those roles can ever do. An **AWS Config rule** is **detective** — it doesn't block anything, it reports compliance after the fact. **Analogy:** SCP = rules for the whole building; permission boundary = a specific employee's access badge limits; Config rule = the inspector who writes up violations later. **Worked example:** you let a dev team create IAM roles but attach a *permission boundary* so none of those roles can ever touch billing — while an *SCP* separately forbids the whole account from leaving approved regions. Predict: which of the three actually *prevents* an action vs. just *reports* it? ||SCPs and permission boundaries prevent (they're ceilings on what's allowed); Config rules only detect and report.||",
