            "title": "The default Compute Engine service account trap",
            "body": "When you enable Compute Engine, GCP auto-creates a **default Compute Engine service account** and, historically, attaches it to new VMs with the `Editor` primitive role and broad scopes. That means a freshly booted VM often carries a near-omnipotent identity. Analogy: every new company car comes pre-loaded with the office master key in the glovebox. Worked example: compromise any such VM, read its attached token from the metadata server, and you are acting with project-wide `Editor`. Predict: why is 'just one web server got popped' often catastrophic in GCP? ||Because the box's default SA frequently holds Editor, so one popped VM can equal project-wide write access.||",
