            "title": "Impersonation chaining",
            "body": "GCP escalation often looks like a chain of impersonations: SA-A can `getAccessToken` on SA-B, which can `actAs` or `signJwt` toward SA-C, which holds the real power. Each hop uses tokens, no passwords. Analogy: a relay race where each runner hands the baton (a short-lived token) to the next, and only the last runner reaches the vault. Worked example: `gcloud --impersonate-service-account=sa-b@proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com ...` then, from sa-b's context, impersonate sa-c. Enumeration tools find these edges automatically. Predict: why are impersonation chains stealthier than stealing a key? ||They use short-lived tokens issued through legitimate IAM APIs, so there is no exported key file and the activity blends into normal generateAccessToken calls.||",
